<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Women's Monument</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1913</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Vrouemonument</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Women's Monument is a memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, unveiled on 16 December 1913. It commemorates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> women and children, some 26,000 by common estimate, who died in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> and bronze sculpture by Anton van Wouw became an important symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> remembrance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument.jpg" alt="National Women's Monument" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlviloria · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloemfontein_Women%27s_Memorial_Vil004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
